# Internationalization (i18n)

Site UI is English today, but code must stay ready for more languages (including possible RTL later). Do **not** build a separate RTL stylesheet or `rtl-` variants now.

## Strings

- Wrap all user-facing theme strings with `__()` / `esc_html__()` / `esc_html_e()` / `esc_attr__()` and text domain `orange-county-handy`.
- No hardcoded English in templates for labels, buttons, empty states, aria-labels, etc.
- Dynamic content from CMS/ACF still goes through `esc_html()` / `esc_url()` as usual.

```php
<?php esc_html_e('Get a quote', 'orange-county-handy'); ?>
<?php echo esc_html(sprintf(__('Hello, %s', 'orange-county-handy'), $name)); ?>
```

## Spacing & alignment — logical properties

Prefer direction-agnostic Tailwind utilities so layout can flip with `dir` later:

| Prefer | Avoid (when mirroring matters) |
|--------|--------------------------------|
| `ms-*` / `me-*` | `ml-*` / `mr-*` |
| `ps-*` / `pe-*` | `pl-*` / `pr-*` |
| `text-start` / `text-end` | `text-left` / `text-right` |
| `start-*` / `end-*` | `left-*` / `right-*` |
| `border-s-*` / `border-e-*` | `border-l-*` / `border-r-*` |

`mx-*`, `px-*`, `gap-*`, `justify-between`, and centered layouts are fine as-is.

## Do not

- Hardcode `dir="ltr"` on wrappers (let WordPress / `language_attributes()` own document direction)
- Ship mirrored duplicate markup or `rtl:`-only forks “just in case”
- Translate CMS/ACF field *values* in PHP — those belong in multilingual plugins later
